{
  "requirement": "`node` identifies the enclosing outline item. `excerpt` holds the text of the matched byte range, which may be smaller than the enclosing item.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct StructuralMatch {\n    pub node: OutlineData,\n    pub excerpt: String,\n}\n\nimpl StructuralMatch {\n    pub fn new(node: OutlineData, excerpt: String) -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.search.StructuralMatch`.\n\n- **`node` identifies the enclosing outline item.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` asserts `node.path == visible.rs` and `node.selector == \"fn[visible]\"` for a `call()` match inside `fn visible`. The Python search asserts the path `script.py`. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` asserts `src/one.rs` and `fn[cached]`.\n- **`excerpt` holds the matched byte range, which may be smaller than the enclosing item.** Both search tests assert `excerpt == \"call()\"`. That text is smaller than the enclosing `fn`, so a whole-item excerpt would fail.\n- **`StructuralMatch::new(node, excerpt)` and the public fields.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/search.rs#fn[callers_can_construct_complete_and_partial_search_results]` builds a match with `new` from outside the crate. It then asserts that `node` equals the supplied address and `excerpt` equals \"example\".\n- **`#[non_exhaustive]`, the public fields and the signatures.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks rustdoc output against the specification. It reaches `check_declaration`, which asserts that any public struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`. It also compares the published signatures with the declared ones.\n\nI found no obligation without a test.",
